\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Running example: the same agentic-memory query as a natural-language ask (left, keywords highlighted) and as an algebraic expression in the extended relational algebra (right). The query exercises two vector columns (\textit  {emb} for body, \textit  {emb\_alt} for summary) plus a tag predicate, a time window, a categorical subsystem filter, a one-hop graph expansion along \textit  {derived\_from}, and a top-$K$ over the materialised distance columns. An explicit score-fusion policy (e.g.\ $0.7\,d_1+0.3\,d_2$) is also expressible via the $\tau $ operator and is not exercised in this example.}}{1}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:threelayer}{{1}{1}{Running example: the same agentic-memory query as a natural-language ask (left, keywords highlighted) and as an algebraic expression in the extended relational algebra (right). The query exercises two vector columns (\textit {emb} for body, \textit {emb\_alt} for summary) plus a tag predicate, a time window, a categorical subsystem filter, a one-hop graph expansion along \textit {derived\_from}, and a top-$K$ over the materialised distance columns. An explicit score-fusion policy (e.g.\ $0.7\,d_1+0.3\,d_2$) is also expressible via the $\tau $ operator and is not exercised in this example}{figure.caption.4}{}}
